// @emit-error emitProgram: parameter `U[]` still names an unsubstituted type parameter — a lambda declared inside a generic body keeps the enclosing `T`, so its signature is interned at the unsubstituted type; give the lambda a concrete parameter type or take the value from the enclosing scope
// THE FLOOR D426 BUILT, AND THE DOMAIN IT STILL OWNS after the close. Per-pin lambda lifting
// (`monoCloneLambdaSubst`) substitutes a lambda's own annotations at every instantiation, so
// the family that floor was built for now RUNS — `lambda-param-type-param-runs.vl` beside
// this file is the accept set. What survives is the case the substitution cannot reach.
//
// `U` IS BOUND BY NOTHING. It is a type parameter of `opT`, so the lambda's `U[]` names an
// enclosing type parameter and the annotation is a template — but no parameter of `opT`
// mentions `U`, so no call site pins it and `monoSubstAnn` has no spelling to substitute.
// The clone declines, the template stays where it is, and the floor names it.
//
// AND THE DECLINE IS WHY THE PRUNE KEEPS TWO LEDGERS. `monomorphize` stubs a lambda template
// once its instances no longer refer to it; if it stubbed this one, the instance body's
// `g(q)` would call a `() -> void` stub — `vl check` rc 0 and a silently wrong answer, which
// is strictly worse than the loud refusal below. `monoLamShared` is the record of a declined
// clone and this fixture is what proves it is read: delete the `monoNoteLamNode` call in
// `monoCloneLambdaSubst`'s `-2` arm and this case stops refusing.

function opT<T, U>(a: T[]): i32 {
  const g = (x: U[]) => x.length
  const q: i32[] = [1, 2]
  return g(q) + a.length
}

function cell(): i32 {
  const a: i32[] = [1]
  return opT(a)
}

print(cell())
